\ A task parked on a `once` cell is woken only by the task computing the value, which happens
\ entirely inside the scheduler. That task is itself parked on a channel with no sender, so nothing
\ can move and the pool is deadlocked.
\
\ The `started` channel orders the two. Its send blocks until the second task takes it, so the
\ second task reaches the cell only once the force is open.

use "testing" ;

\ `<channel>` needs a task-scope, so both are built inside the scope below and left here for the
\ body to reach.
gates: V{ } ;
started: ( -- ch ) [ gates 0 #nth ] ;
blocked: ( -- ch ) [ gates 1 #nth ] ;

held: ( -- fixnum ) once [ t started send blocked receive drop 1 ] ;

[
  <channel> gates swap #push! drop
  <channel> gates swap #push! drop

  [ held drop ] spawn drop
  [ started receive drop held drop ] spawn drop
] task-scope
